@title Orders platform architecture
@subtitle Kestrel Outfitters · Owner: Commerce Platform team · Last reviewed 2026-10-01

# Overview

The Orders platform takes web orders, reserves stock and charges cards. It handles about 3,200 orders per hour at peak.

# Architecture diagram

diagram orders dir=LR title="Orders platform"
orders: cust "Customers" user
orders: stripe "Stripe" ext
orders: alb "Application Load Balancer" lb
orders: osvc "Orders Service" svc "ECS Fargate, 6 tasks" focus
orders: isvc "Inventory Service" svc "ECS Fargate, 4 tasks"
orders: db "Orders DB" db "RDS PostgreSQL 16, Multi-AZ"
orders: evq "Order Events" queue "SQS"
orders: ecr "ECR Registry" store
orders: cust -> alb : HTTPS
orders: alb -> osvc : /orders
orders: alb -> isvc : /inventory
orders: osvc -> db : SQL
orders: osvc -> evq : publish
orders: evq -> isvc : poll
orders: osvc -> stripe : charge
orders: ecr -> osvc : image pull
orders: group vpc "Orders VPC 10.20.0.0/16" alb, osvc, isvc, db
orders: group prod "Prod account" vpc, evq
orders: group shared "Shared services account" ecr

# Components

table = Component,Runtime,Purpose; Orders Service,ECS Fargate with 6 tasks,Accepts orders and charges cards; Inventory Service,ECS Fargate with 4 tasks,Reserves stock; Orders DB,RDS PostgreSQL 16 on db.r6g.xlarge; Multi-AZ,Stores orders; Order Events,Standard SQS queue with 14 day retention,Carries order events to inventory

# Failure modes

!! An Orders DB Multi-AZ failover takes about 60 seconds. During that window, order writes fail and the storefront shows a retry banner.
